 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure of the check aborts it with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files are still
+  only warned about (exit 0); the check does not turn them into a failure.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e` so that internal errors are swallowed again.
+- Reason: the maintainer found the check too forgiving — breakage inside it went unnoticed. The user overrode
+  §check-fails-open explicitly (task request, 2026-10-23), accepting the risk described there.
+- Note: guard commands whose non-zero status is normal (e.g. use `if`, not `[ … ] && …`, as the last command of a
+  loop in a command substitution) so `-e` does not abort on a healthy run.
+
+### §check-fails-open — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
